    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d424">

                <pb facs="0504" n="400" sameAs="frus:frus1932v05/pg400"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.00 Revolutions/260: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Thurston</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-07-16T15:00:00-04:00">July 16, 1932—3
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>49. Your 62, July 14, 2 p.m. If Santos is in the control of insurgents the
                    Brazilian Government would have no right to close this port by decree as
                    reported in your telegram of July 12, 9 a.m., unless this decree is enforced by
                    an effective blockade. As it appears from <pb facs="0505" n="401" xml:id="pg401"
                    /> your telegram 65, of July 15, that the Brazilian authorities have given
                    clearance to the <hi rendition="#italic">Angeles</hi> and the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Delsud</hi>, no occasion is presented for determining
                    the legal effect of the foregoing decree. If, however, on a future occasion the
                    Brazilian Government should attempt in pursuance of this decree to interfere
                    with an American ship attempting to enter or leave Santos, you should endeavor
                    immediately to ascertain whether an effective blockade is maintained in order to
                    determine whether an occasion for protest may have arisen.</p>
                <p>The Department considers it inexpedient for you to obtain any formal opinion from
                    the Foreign Office on this subject as indicated in paragraph 2 of your telegram
                    No. 62.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
